  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Anomalously warm low-level thicknesses beneath \nanomalous ridging aloft will continue to keep temperatures well\nabove normal today. Another Heat Advisory has been issued for \nmost of ENC, with heat index values peaking at 105-109 deg this \nafternoon. Expanded the Heat Advisory to the immediate coast \nand northern Outer Banks. \n\nFriday continues to have the strongest signal for the peak of \nthis week's heat wave, with highs approaching 100 degrees across\nthe coastal plain, and low to mid 90s along the coast. Even \nwith another strong seabreeze and southerly onshore flow, it \nshould get hot enough to reach Heat Advisory criteria even along\nthe coast. Of note, some inland locations may flirt with \nExtreme Heat Warning criteria. Regardless, this will continue to\nbe a notable, and unusual, late-season heat wave for all of \nENC.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong upper level trough will develop off \nthe U.S. East Coast over the weekend, and this will help drive a\nstrong cold front south through the Carolinas on Saturday. \nStrong heating of a moist boundary layer ahead of the front \nshould allow moderate to potentially strong instability to \ndevelop from the Mid-Atlantic south into North Carolina on \nFriday. While some residual subsidence/capping may be present \nbeneath the ridge, especially across NC, guidance show a decent \nsignal for convection developing upstream of ENC, and making a \nrun towards the coast during the evening or overnight hours. \nWhile the pattern is supportive of severe weather, the timing is\nless favorable for ENC, and there is added uncertainty \nregarding any residual influence of the ridge. Consequently, \nmachine learning guidance have actually backed off some on the \nsevere weather potential on Friday, which lowers overall \nconfidence. \n\nOn Saturday, the ridge will continue to get suppressed further \nsouth and west with less influence across the Carolinas. This \nplus increased forcing along the cold front should support an \nimproved risk of deep convection, and less limiting factors, \ncompared to Friday. Interestingly, machine learning guidance has\npulled back some on Saturday as well, and this may be related \nto some uncertainty regarding the timing of the front and how \nmuch instability can develop along/ahead of it. Of the two days,\nthough, Saturday appears to have a better chance than Friday. \nWe'll continue to monitor the potential over both days. In \ngeneral, the setup favors clusters of thunderstorms, perhaps an \nMCS or two, impacting the area with strong to damaging wind \ngusts. \n\nBehind the front, temperatures and humidity will get knocked \nback down closer to normal for early September, with more \ncomfortable conditions lasting into early next week.",
